The npm read-through cache (also called the npm proxy cache) puts a package registry proxy close
to your [Workers](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution).
The first time a package is requested, it is fetched from the public npm registry and stored in the
cache.
Repeated installs of the same package are then served from the cache instead of the upstream
registry, cutting install time and external network usage.

It also protects your organization from npm registry outages.

## Enable the cache

The cache runs inside the cluster that hosts your Workers, so how you get it depends on your Nx
Cloud deployment:

- **Dedicated compute cluster**: the cache is an Nx Cloud add-on that runs on a
  [dedicated compute cluster](/docs/kb/dedicated-compute-cluster).
  Once the cluster is active, open [**Settings > Add-ons**](https://cloud.nx.app/go/organization/add-ons)
  for your organization.
  On the **Dedicated compute cluster** card, find **npm read-through cache**, click
  **Request add-on**, and confirm.
- **Single-tenant**: the cache is available for hosted
  [single-tenant deployments](/docs/enterprise/single-tenant/overview).
  [Contact the Nx team](https://nx.dev/enterprise) to add it to your deployment.

## Cache address

Each deployment exposes the cache at a fixed address inside the cluster.
Use the address for your deployment wherever `<cache-url>` appears on this page:

| Deployment                | Cache address                              |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| Dedicated compute cluster | `http://npm:4873/`                         |
| Single-tenant             | `http://cache.npm.svc.cluster.local:4873/` |

Both addresses resolve only inside the cluster, so use them from Workers, not from a local machine.

## Scope

- **Public npm packages only.** The cache proxies the public npm registry.
- **Private and scoped packages that require authentication are not cached.** Send them directly
  to your private registry, as described in [route private packages](#route-private-packages).
- **Do not publish packages to the cache.** See [configure package publishing](#configure-package-publishing).

## Configure the default registry

Point your package manager at the cache before it installs dependencies.
Put the file at the workspace root or in the Worker home directory:

{% tabs syncKey="package-manager" %}
{% tabitem label="npm, pnpm, and Yarn Classic" %}

npm, pnpm, and Yarn Classic read the registry from `.npmrc`:

```ini
# .npmrc
registry=<cache-url>
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% tabitem label="Yarn Modern" %}

Yarn Modern ignores `.npmrc` and reads the registry from `npmRegistryServer` in `.yarnrc.yml`:

```yaml
# .yarnrc.yml
npmRegistryServer: '<cache-url>'
```

{% /tabitem %}
{% /tabs %}

If an existing project-level file sets the registry, update that file.
A user-level setting does not override a project-level setting.

## Route private packages

Map each private package scope to the registry that hosts it.
The scoped setting takes precedence over the default cache registry:

```ini
# .npmrc
registry=<cache-url>
@<package-scope>:registry=https://<private-registry-host>/npm/
```

You can also write the scoped setting with the npm CLI:

```shell
npm config set @<package-scope>:registry https://<private-registry-host>/npm/
```

## Configure package publishing

Set a publishing registry for each package that your workspace publishes.
The `publishConfig` field in `package.json` keeps publish requests away from the cache:

```json
{
  "publishConfig": {
    "registry": "https://<publishing-registry-host>/npm/"
  }
}
```

You can also pass `npm publish --registry <url>`.
A scoped registry in `.npmrc` also applies when you publish a package from that scope.

## Configure an agent setup step

Add the registry settings before the dependency installation step in your
[launch template](/docs/kb/launch-templates):

```yaml
# .nx/pipelines/ci.yaml
launch-templates:
  linux-medium:
    init-steps:
      - name: Configure the npm read-through cache
        script: |
          npm config set --location=user registry <cache-url>
          npm config set --location=user @<package-scope>:registry https://<private-registry-host>/npm/
```

Use a committed project-level configuration instead when the workspace already defines its
registry in `.npmrc` or an equivalent file.

## Understand cache behavior

The cache uses multiple replicas for reliability and local network access.
A cold replica still fetches a package from the upstream registry, so the first request in an
availability zone can take longer than later requests.

For package manager details, see the [npm `.npmrc` reference](https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/configuring-npm/npmrc),
[pnpm settings](https://pnpm.io/npmrc), [Yarn Classic configuration](https://classic.yarnpkg.com/lang/en/docs/cli/config/),
and [Yarn Modern configuration](https://yarnpkg.com/configuration/yarnrc).
